            <ArticleTitle>Body mass index in blunt trauma patients with hemorrhagic shock: opposite ends of the body mass index spectrum portend poor outcome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>659-65</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.amjsurg.2014.12.016</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0002-9610(15)00009-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">There are controversial data on the relationship between trauma and body mass index. We investigated this relationship in traumatic hemorrhagic shock.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The &quot;Glue Grant&quot; database was analyzed, stratifying patients into underweight, normal weight (NW), overweight, Class I obesity, Class II obesity, and Class III obesity. Predictors of mortality and surgical interventions were statistically determined.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">One thousand nine hundred seventy-six patients were included with no difference in injury severity between groups. Marshall's score was elevated in overweight (5.3 ± 2.7, P = .016), Class I obesity (5.8 ± 2.7, P &lt; .001), Class II obesity (5.9 ± 2.8, P &lt; .001), and Class III obesity (6.3 ± 3.0, P &lt; .001) compared with NW (4.8 ± 2.6). Underweight had higher lactate (4.8 ± 4.2 vs 3.3 ± 2.5, P = .04), were 4 times more likely to die (odds ratio 3.87, confidence interval 2.22 to 6.72), and were more likely to undergo a laparotomy (odds ratio 2.06, confidence interval 1.31 to 3.26) than NW.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Early assessment of body mass index, with active management of complications in each class, may reduce mortality in traumatic hemorrhagic shock.</AbstractText>
